WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.520
Choose local models by hardware fit, task fit, memory, speed, quality, stability, privacy,

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.450
and operational needs.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:10.260
No parameter count, benchmark, or model family is universally best.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:16.020
Inventory CPU, GPU, VRAM, unified memory, system RAM, storage, acceleration support,

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.000
power, and cooling.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.730
Evaluate the complete model, quantization, runtime, context, offload,

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.500
and concurrency configuration.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.965
Smaller models can be faster, stable, and excellent for focused work.

00:00:25.965 --> 00:00:29.995
Larger models may improve difficult tasks but increase memory, latency, power,

00:00:29.995 --> 00:00:31.500
and deployment complexity.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.910
Quantization and context reshape those tradeoffs.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:38.435
Benchmark representative prompts repeatedly and record quality, refusals, hallucinations,

00:00:38.435 --> 00:00:42.220
first-token delay, prompt speed, generation, memory, thermals, failures,

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.470
and workflow success.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:47.415
Start with a smaller reliable model, then scale when measured shortcomings justify it.

00:00:47.415 --> 00:00:51.425
The best local model is the one that performs acceptably on the actual machine for the

00:00:51.425 --> 00:00:53.490
actual tasks with enough margin to operate.
